on" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465187} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006c4bcd-eb9a-5cf9-a1c3-264a03153e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464508} {:answer/id #uuid "2a3e64a4-3d90-513a-aabe-b9c6b1f73717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "006c4bcd-eb9a-5cf9-a1c3-264a03153e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464508} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5d092e3-b11d-5fd2-902b-f74605931fec" :answer-field/key "$L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465188} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75702486-4350-5a91-a8d6-8dfdbb2ce100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668431} {:answer/id #uuid "de1ee197-65cb-5fa1-9897-8719201beed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de1ee197-65cb-5fa1-9897-8719201beed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668432} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e316fb5-43b4-5727-9714-64602ca9f022" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "21a6de96-a543-5af1-a71d-fa101cbb68c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-157392" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,-1,1)$ and $(3,0,-1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{2}=y=\\frac{z+1}{-2}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)-3}{2}\\ne (0)\\ne \\frac{(0)+1}{-2}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is false. Let's consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(4,-1,0)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n\\frac{(4)-3}{2}\\ne (-1)\\ne \\frac{(0)+1}{-2}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is false. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7abbf7e3-3d54-5120-940b-09c5e185eeda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464510} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf4fa89-8708-540b-8885-0c47fdd3e072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cf4fa89-8708-540b-8885-0c47fdd3e072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464511} :answer-field/id #uuid "557f3350-055d-5356-ae63-38b2200cd37a" :answer-field/key "$Π$ contains the zero-vector" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465189} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5c5cfe7-8966-5eae-8257-b7176adfa493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464512} {:answer/id #uuid "9903f099-adbf-5217-b6de-78c54266cd8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9903f099-adbf-5217-b6de-78c54266cd8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464513} :answer-field/id #uuid "330de474-2c09-5c0b-bf01-776c3b12aa06" :answer-field/key "$Π$ is closed under vector addition" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465190} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83483ad5-c53b-598d-8a53-972a1e72c631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464514} {:answer/id #uuid "d31bafaf-13b0-54db-8f23-7a5c4064f4d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d31bafaf-13b0-54db-8f23-7a5c4064f4d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464515} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7929bb8-48e8-5ad5-a9f9-a247e206819e" :answer-field/key "$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465191} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26a5a7e9-ab0e-54c8-8d41-e44b3cc2bfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464516} {:answer/id #uuid "d18b82a3-c24b-5e6a-aa60-1381535b5797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26a5a7e9-ab0e-54c8-8d41-e44b3cc2bfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464516} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bdeead7-751f-5041-8724-a9d669e4d63b" :answer-field/key "$Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "42d6a94c-6a33-5351-adc5-bba146953de0" :question/math-academy-id "q-157463" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=2x-y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ 2x-y\\end{bmatrix},\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$Π$ contains the zero-vector” is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=2x-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2x-y \\\\ (0) & =2(0)-(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ (2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2})+(2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 2({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})-({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=2x-y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k(2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ 2(k{a}_{1})-k{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=2x-y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is false. Since statements “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” and “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$Π$ contains the zero-vector”; “$Π$ is closed under vector addition”; “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c4e438f-df53-5f70-8e5d-7a169145d53c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464518} {:answer/id #uuid "af822d08-9b9b-57a7-a8c8-33883456d16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af822d08-9b9b-57a7-a8c8-33883456d16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464519} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb16460b-15cc-5a45-acb1-fc84c3e266eb" :answer-field/key "$Π$ contains the zero-vector" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465193} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c02e032e-2b5a-5921-b21e-d772b83f8b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464520} {:answer/id #uuid "7b351d6f-31f6-5d4a-b904-75ae0914c6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b351d6f-31f6-5d4a-b904-75ae0914c6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464521} :answer-field/id #uuid "31556c67-692f-5001-8e1d-96f75b44588a" :answer-field/key "$Π$ is closed under vector addition" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465194} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1287fcc-3fcb-510a-b75c-41ef53051154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464522} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ab104e-08b0-5836-a2c0-cb9cfa04791d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3ab104e-08b0-5836-a2c0-cb9cfa04791d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464523} :answer-field/id #uuid "f71fabb8-3089-5b76-9692-7fbacfd573c7" :answer-field/key "$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465195} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55db86b2-c488-5f67-9c04-0a4b0afc906c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464524} {:answer/id #uuid "de642d2b-0e51-5ff8-9f66-d4e26ea5ccdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de642d2b-0e51-5ff8-9f66-d4e26ea5ccdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464525} :answer-field/id #uuid "06fe3143-439a-5767-94b9-14c70cde2a7d" :answer-field/key "$Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "19a5b6b6-a775-5cf8-81ee-dfec54297bbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-157464" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=x+2y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ x+2y\\end{bmatrix},\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$Π$ contains the zero-vector” is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=x+2y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+2y \\\\ (0) & =(0)+2(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}+2{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})+2({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+2y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k({a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k{a}_{1}+2(k{a}_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+2y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is true. Since statements “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” and “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98fdad6c-bf29-53b5-8c45-6b7cb4fe8703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449786} {:answer/id #uuid "d319819e-c3e4-5493-bd8e-415384a14552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98fdad6c-bf29-53b5-8c45-6b7cb4fe8703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449786} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd61d60f-6318-5dc1-ba28-adf5f624114c" :answer-field/key "$Π$ contains the zero-vector" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450847} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ca43660-1795-5c4c-a9f9-516cab85f57f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449788} {:answer/id #uuid "b0cc69b5-c14e-5b6b-89a2-bbeb398480fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449789}] :an